{"title":"Power set","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>power set</b> of a given <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Set\" title=\"Set\">set</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/1/d51deaac5e024665f8d409987d79bf82bf618f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{P}(S)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"18\" /> of all <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Subset\" title=\"Subset\">subsets</a> of that set. It is also sometimes denoted by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/5/4759f99bb09ab92276fa04f740b863b509e772a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^S$\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Examples\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Examples</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Size_Comparison\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Size Comparison</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Size_for_Finite_Sets\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Size for Finite Sets</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Method_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Method 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Method_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Method 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Method_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Method 3</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#External_Links\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">External Links</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Examples\">Examples</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Power_set&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Examples\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Empty_set\" title=\"Empty set\">empty set</a> has only one subset, itself.  Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/f/64fc73da0d610358453b366274e6c9bb4bcf2dd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{P}(\\emptyset) = \\{\\emptyset\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"19\" />.\n</p><p>A set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/c/6bca7f68c5ef65ce19007f70e9fcc46262f6f81f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{a\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" /> with a single element has two subsets, the empty set and the entire set.  Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/3/29393dcbf1761e28aa4e6d0b9d71119d2c43f93e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{P}(\\{a\\}) = \\{\\emptyset, \\{a\\}\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"142\" height=\"19\" />.\n</p><p>A set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/c/bac9a10dd068d99753bfce956841aecf0af0c83a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{a, b\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> with two elements has four subsets, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/0/57055675d64f121854ba85e1a1b18ae22d497240.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{P}(\\{a, b\\}) = \\{\\emptyset, \\{a\\}, \\{b\\}, \\{a, b\\}\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"244\" height=\"19\" />.\n</p><p>Similarly, for any <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Finite\" title=\"Finite\">finite</a> set with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> elements, the power set has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c36f0b157e8c2fbe0638c2f2b378e08d07695aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> elements.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Size_Comparison\">Size Comparison</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Power_set&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Size Comparison\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that for any <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nonnegative\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Nonnegative\">nonnegative</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integer\" title=\"Integer\">integer</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/e/d2e279c991ae404fda50ca64e89097780dbd14ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n &gt; n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"53\" height=\"13\" /> and so for any finite set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/8/6589326849a04eacb08b4b92c7dc9282df324d2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|\\mathcal P (S)| &gt; |S|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" /> (where <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Absolute_value\" title=\"Absolute value\">absolute value</a> signs here denote the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cardinality\" title=\"Cardinality\">cardinality</a> of a set).  The analogous result is also true for <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinite\" title=\"Infinite\">infinite</a> sets (and thus for all sets): for any set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, the cardinality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/1/3910a127d56605dc37c0bcea41c23a5740407b19.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|\\mathcal P (S)|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"48\" height=\"18\" /> of the power set is strictly larger than the cardinality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/c/11ceba9d7455309f60178fe82bb6e81e6c01fa83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|S|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" /> of the set itself.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Power_set&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>There is a natural <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Injection\" title=\"Injection\">injection</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/8/6a8ea7d426bbfc59faf565d58d0e5ce5b5dc102c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S \\hookrightarrow \\mathcal P (S)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"81\" height=\"18\" /> taking <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/2/e423fcf8923420e6b8b17e96416f9832a1e0baf7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\mapsto \\{x\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/9/f19ead8d605fd21f7dda7c5e725fb0defceb6352.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|S| \\leq |\\mathcal P(S)|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" />.  \nSuppose for the sake of contradiction that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/b/dcbc2d75f42abd5d1148bf14c7d7a4718c667eb0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|S| = |\\mathcal P(S)|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" />.  Then there is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Bijection\" title=\"Bijection\">bijection</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/7/5c787b5f43aa4570ba2beadbb8e380a3805e8c2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f: \\mathcal P(S) \\to S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"107\" height=\"18\" />.  Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/a/baac0f4d79e78b2ce11633b027811ca1ad2ff660.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T \\subset S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" /> be defined by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/0/dc0cc6f06756dccb3c66af72ab031b765f0a1c8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T = \\{x \\in S \\;|\\; x \\not\\in f(x) \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"182\" height=\"18\" />.  Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/d/16d99202492dcf0ad44d370c9055c493b397e988.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T \\in \\mathcal P(S)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" /> and since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is a bijection, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/3/9939c076aaea4bd87da338ebdf8ce089b401666a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\exists y\\in S \\;|\\; T = f(y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"139\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Now, note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/e/aeef0c4c5babcfbc7dc2b066088c2b5409e6c642.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y \\in T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"47\" height=\"18\" /> by definition if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/4/4c46c5d6362326c99ff2b86d7fe7e2f419b73627.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y \\not\\in f(y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"18\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/e/aeef0c4c5babcfbc7dc2b066088c2b5409e6c642.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y \\in T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"47\" height=\"18\" /> if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/4/624f32aa0cd043146edf5d1b0b3b186c81b59fa3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y \\not \\in T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" />.  This is a clear contradiction.  Thus the bijection <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> cannot really exist and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/919308a9875bc05ce171c3c996672044962ca44e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|\\mathcal P (S)| \\neq |S|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/d/0bd67fccc51314646567f89036a73a928d5b918c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|\\mathcal P(S)| &gt; |S|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" />, as desired.\n</p><p><br />\nNote that this proof does not rely upon either the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Continuum_Hypothesis&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Continuum Hypothesis (page does not exist)\">Continuum Hypothesis</a> or the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Axiom_of_Choice\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Axiom of Choice\">Axiom of Choice</a>.  It is a good example of a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Diagonal_argument&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Diagonal argument (page does not exist)\">diagonal argument</a>, a method pioneered by the mathematician <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Georg_Cantor\" title=\"Georg Cantor\">Georg Cantor</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Size_for_Finite_Sets\">Size for Finite Sets</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Power_set&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Size for Finite Sets\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The number of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Element\" title=\"Element\">elements</a> in a <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">power set</a> of a set with n elements is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c36f0b157e8c2fbe0638c2f2b378e08d07695aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> for all finite sets. This can be proven in a number of ways:\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Method_1\">Method 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Power_set&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Method 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Either an element in the power set can have 0 elements, one element, ... , or n elements. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/d/94d9345ff1cd9bc5a898c3401b9821bcbe329360.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{n}{0}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"35\" height=\"53\" /> ways to have no elements, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/7/a673cb59b9f1f4b0f822a01b3e1bb7217ac870f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{n}{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"41\" height=\"55\" /> ways to have one element, ... , and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/e/36e3bc5d9f9987175824c3c27dbaf8f6cc2072a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{n}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"35\" height=\"53\" /> ways to have n elements. We add:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/5/b459f24f1d489fbaed26c0e3d5579a3dcaae6fc7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{n}{0}+\\binom{n}{1}+\\cdots+\\binom{n}{n}=2^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"246\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>as desired.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Method_2\">Method 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Power_set&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Method 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We proceed with <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Induction\" title=\"Induction\">induction</a>.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be the set with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> elements. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/3/02349576cee613512cdf301a365f06c0760acab5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is the empty set. Then\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/b/acbe60f4f020eead8c3522c8f753a21f46e304fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(S)=\\{\\emptyset \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>and has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/8/3688876ff545b6900b80c429336386b50e4a4326.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^0=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"49\" height=\"15\" /> element.\n</p><p><br />\nNow let's say that the theorem stated above is true or n=k. We shall prove it for k+1.\n</p><p>Let's say that Q has k+1 elements.\n</p><p>In set Q, if we leave element x out, there will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/7/767b7dc4a8df95a9f8a0faa3bb155e7b49943407.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"17\" /> elements in the power set. Now we include the sets that do include x. But that's just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/7/767b7dc4a8df95a9f8a0faa3bb155e7b49943407.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"17\" />, since we are choosing either 0 1, ... or k elements to go with x. Therefore, if there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/7/767b7dc4a8df95a9f8a0faa3bb155e7b49943407.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"17\" /> elements in the power set of a set that has k elements, then there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/3/473b67382183fc823e117858fef19aaf2ec23948.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{k+1}$\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" /> elements in the power set of a set that has k+1 elements.\n</p><p>Therefore, the number of elements in a power set of a set with n elements is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c36f0b157e8c2fbe0638c2f2b378e08d07695aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Method_3\">Method 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Power_set&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Method 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We demonstrated in Method 2 that if S is the empty set, it works.\n</p><p>Now let's say that S has at least one element.\n</p><p>For an element x, it can be either in or out of a subset. Since there are n elements, and each different choice of in/out leads to a different subset, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c36f0b157e8c2fbe0638c2f2b378e08d07695aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^n$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> elements in the power sum.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Power_set&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Set_theory\" title=\"Set theory\">Set theory</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Set\" title=\"Set\">Set</a></li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"External_Links\">External Links</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Power_set&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: External Links\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://mathworld.wolfram.com/PowerSet.html\">Power Set</a> at Wolfram MathWorld.</li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018103557\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.030 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.047 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 302/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 9155/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:3850:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018103557 and revision id 93025. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}